// @check
// @error wrong number of arguments: expected 1, got 2
// @error argument 1: expected u8[], got string
// @error argument 1: expected u8[], got i32
// CONTRACT (must-error): the filesystem intrinsics are ORDINARY declared functions to the
// checker — `declare("__fs_stat__", mkFuncTy([u8Arr], TY_I32))` and friends — so arity and
// argument types are checked by the same path every user call takes, with the same
// messages. Nothing here is a special-cased arm, which is the point: the signature IS the
// contract and there is no second place for it to be spelled differently.
//
// A PATH IS BYTES, not a string, and that is the rejection worth pinning. `u8[]` is what
// POSIX actually stores and what a file's contents actually are; turning a `string` into
// those bytes is `std:utf8`'s job and is a DECODING decision with a policy attached
// (strict or lossy), not something this layer should make silently at a call boundary.
//
// The emitter carries its own arity guard as well (`fsIntrinsicArity`), so a call that
// somehow reached lowering with the wrong shape fails loudly there too rather than
// building a stack the module cannot validate. It is unreachable while the checker
// agrees, and free when it does.
const p: u8[] = [46]
print(__fs_stat__(p, p))
print(__fs_stat__("."))
print(__fs_write__(0, p))
